Statement risks and benefits of the procedure, and potential alternatives. All questions were answered, and patient expressed understanding and agreement to proceed.

Upon entry through the oropharynx, the endoscope was carefully advanced through the upper esophageal sphincter. The esophagus was inspected: mucosa was normal in color and texture. The Z-line was noted at 40 cm from the incisors, with a normal gastroesophageal junction (GEJ). There were no rings or strictures observed.

In the stomach, the fundus, body, and antrum appeared normal under insufflation. Rugal folds were distinct, with mild erythema noted in the antral region. No erosions or ulcers were identified. The pylorus was patent.

Examination of the duodenal bulb and second portion revealed normal mucosal pattern with visible villi. There was no evidence of inflammation or ulcers.

Retroflexion in the stomach showed a well-demarcated cardia and fundus. Findings * Mild erythema in the gastric antrum, approximately 3-4 mm patches. * Normal appearing duodenal mucosa.

Microscopic Description: Sections show mild chronic inflammation with scattered inflammatory cells within the lamina propria. No Helicobacter pylori organisms identified.

Recovery Course Patient recovered uneventfully. No significant pain or nausea reported. Tolerated oral intake without issues. Vitals remained stable throughout recovery period.

